A||N||7||Dan86gt3.8||having major suspension problems!||||1||
Z||000000||Dan86gt3.8||12-11-2000||01:35 AM||||Ok right now when I hit 70 + on the interstate my car jerks left or right if I don't hold the wheel frimly in one spott.  when I change lanes  the car won't glide over when I barely move the wheel it jerks over.  I replaced a rear wheel bearing about a month and a half ago and got it re-aligned.  Sinse then I bottomed the front suspension out going through a big dip.  Umm I also noticed my front wheels push (when the whells turn but the car goes straight)really easi;y when it rains.  I am not a dumbass ethier I am talking about even when I slow down and adjust for the rain my front wheels push where as they used to grip.  So does it sound like I am out of alignment ?  Is the rack bad?  are the shocks bad? tie rods? front ball joints?  Is it something that can be diagnossed hear or is it best taken it to sears or a tire shop that speacializes in front end/suspension/alignment  work?  <P>well thanks for any ideas/solutions to my problems in advance!<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>1/4- 15.3@90mph||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-11-2000||01:48 AM||||It sounds like lower balljoints.  They are easy to check, just jack the car up off of the ground and grab the wheel/tire at the 6 & 12 o'clock position and push/pull (wiggle it).  While doing this look at the balljoints and tie rod.....if anything moves or shifts then it needs replacing.  <P>If you notice no movement in these areas, but there is still play in the wheel while wiggling then it is likely bad wheel bearings.<P>I personally think your lower bal joingts are the culprit.....they wear faster than anything else in the front suspension (bushings a close second).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-11-2000||02:09 AM||||To roughly see how much your alignment is off, take a tape measure and measure the front tires. Outside edge to outside edge as high up as you can go. Then do the same for the backside of the front tires. This will let you know if you are toed in or toed out. Toed in could be bent tierod when you bottomed out. Another thing you could do is raise front of car and remove tires. Then turn steering wheel and look at the ball joint bolts.  You can also check the ball joints by seeing if rotor will wiggle the ball joints. One last thing, look at the top ball joint, if it is aftermarket with slotted holes, you could have shifted it when you bottomed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Stoic||12-11-2000||08:12 AM||||My car was recently acting the same way, I checked the balljoints (as described by mrfiero) and sure enough, thats what it was...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||12-11-2000||09:34 AM||||Having the rear a little bit out of alignment can cause a lot of what you are experiencing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan86gt3.8||12-11-2000||09:52 AM||||How about the wheels pushing in the rain?  Do I need to just slow down or is that a syptom of something wacky?  I will probably check this stuff when i go home for schristmas (if I don't fly off the road on the way there)!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan86gt3.8||12-11-2000||09:56 AM||||ohhh BTW thnaks for the hints!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||12-11-2000||10:12 AM||||If your alignment is out of whack it will cause the car to push in slick weather. Actually if driven in dry weather long enough it could wear out the tires quickly. ||||reg||1
